1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,135
The most distant thing humans have ever
built is about to be one full day away.

2
00:00:06,135 --> 00:00:08,065
At the speed of light.

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:17,408
On the eighteenth of November, twenty twenty-six, by NASA's
calculation, Voyager 1 reaches one light-day from Earth.

4
00:00:17,408 --> 00:00:20,654
Nearly twenty-six billion kilometres.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,531
Light crosses to the Moon
in just over a second.

6
00:00:29,531 --> 00:00:34,091
To the Sun, in eight minutes.
To Neptune, in about four hours.

7
00:00:34,091 --> 00:00:36,356
To Voyager: twenty-four.

8
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:45,494
It left on the fifth of September, nineteen
seventy-seven, on a Titan rocket from Cape Canaveral.

9
00:00:45,494 --> 00:00:48,518
A machine built to last five years.

10
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:57,066
Eighteen months later it swept past Jupiter,
and found volcanoes erupting on the moon Io:

11
00:00:57,066 --> 00:01:01,254
the first active volcanoes
ever seen beyond Earth.

12
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,741
In November nineteen eighty, Saturn.

13
00:01:05,741 --> 00:01:13,203
A close pass by its giant moon, Titan, bent Voyager's
path up and out of the plane of the planets.

14
00:01:13,203 --> 00:01:15,607
No more worlds lay ahead.

15
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,893
But in nineteen ninety, at the urging of the astronomer
Carl Sagan, its cameras were turned around one last time.

16
00:01:25,893 --> 00:01:32,680
From six billion kilometres away, Earth was a
point of light, smaller than a single pixel.

17
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:36,830
A pale blue dot, Sagan called it.

18
00:01:36,830 --> 00:01:39,585
A mote of dust, suspended in a sunbeam.

19
00:01:39,585 --> 00:01:44,251
Half an hour later, the cameras
were switched off for good.

20
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:48,378
Voyager kept going.

21
00:01:48,378 --> 00:01:57,154
In August twenty twelve, it crossed the boundary where the
wind from the Sun gives way to the thin gas between the stars.

22
00:01:57,154 --> 00:02:02,006
It became the first human-made
object in interstellar space.

23
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:06,795
Bolted to its side is a message.

24
00:02:06,795 --> 00:02:13,319
A gold-plated copper record, with instructions
for playing it engraved on the cover.

25
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:27,631
It holds a hundred and fifteen images, greetings in fifty-five languages, the sounds of
surf, thunder, birdsong and whales, and ninety minutes of music, from Bach to Chuck Berry.

26
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,404
And Voyager is still talking to us.

27
00:02:32,404 --> 00:02:36,250
Its transmitter has a power
of about twenty-three watts.

28
00:02:36,250 --> 00:02:39,634
Roughly that of a refrigerator light bulb.

29
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:48,014
By the time that signal reaches Earth, it is far
weaker than a billionth of a billionth of a watt.

30
00:02:48,014 --> 00:02:55,743
To hear it, NASA's Deep Space Network uses dishes seventy
metres across, in California, Spain and Australia.

31
00:02:55,743 --> 00:03:00,537
The data trickles in at a hundred
and sixty bits per second.

32
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:10,279
Its electricity comes from the heat of decaying
plutonium, and every year there are about four watts less.

33
00:03:10,279 --> 00:03:13,987
So the team has been switching
things off, one by one.

34
00:03:13,987 --> 00:03:17,920
Of ten science instruments,
two are still running.

35
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:25,397
This year, engineers shut down one more, and
reworked the power system to buy some time.

36
00:03:25,397 --> 00:03:28,213
Every command now takes a day to arrive.

37
00:03:28,213 --> 00:03:31,671
The answer takes another day to come back.

38
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:36,416
So here is the scale of it.

39
00:03:36,416 --> 00:03:41,597
Almost fifty years of flight, at about
seventeen kilometres every second.

40
00:03:41,597 --> 00:03:45,078
And the distance covered is one light-day.

41
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:50,161
The nearest star is more
than four light-years away.

42
00:03:50,161 --> 00:03:52,978
That is over fifteen hundred light-days.

43
00:03:52,978 --> 00:03:58,899
At Voyager's speed, the journey would
take about seventy-five thousand years.

44
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:03,221
And Voyager is not even heading that way.

45
00:04:03,221 --> 00:04:10,691
In about forty thousand years, it will drift within
a light-year and a half of another star, and sail on.

46
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:20,632
Probably within the next ten years, the power
will fall too low, and Voyager will go quiet.

47
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:26,486
But it will not stop. Nothing
out there will slow it down.

48
00:04:26,486 --> 00:04:31,315
And the record on its side is
expected to last a billion years.

49
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:37,787
One light-day from home. And still going.

